  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 100 PM Saturday... \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Cold temperatures expected again tonight. Freeze \nWarning remains in effect for portions of central NC while the \ngrowing season has ended elsewhere.  \n\nEarly afternoon water vapor imagery continues to show an upper low \nover southeastern Ontario , with broad cyclonic flow extending as \nfar west as the western Great Lakes. At the surface, a 1026+ mb \nsurface ridge is located over the northern Plains and is slowly \nbuilding eastward, although weak troughing is still noted east of \nthe mountains as of late morning. With the arrival of daytime \nheating and steepening low level lapse rates, winds have increased \nout of the northwest between 10-15 mph with occasional gusts of 20-\n25 mph. Cloud cover has also increased quite a but across the \nwestern Piedmont as varying amounts of stratocu have either made it \nover the mountains or developed downwind. \n\nOccasional breeziness should continue through late afternoon before \nwinds drop off considerably before 00Z. Meanwhile, skies should also \nclear out later this evening with the loss of heating. Combined with \nthe eastward progressing surface ridge, this will set the stage for \nthe coldest night of the season so far. Widespread near or below \nfreezing temperatures can be expected early Sunday morning with lows \nranging from the mid/upper 20s to lower 30s, and the Freeze Warning \nwill remain in place for portions of the area. We did move forward \nwith ending the Frost/Freeze program for several counties in central \nNC based on this morning's observed low temperatures (and \nsubsequently removing those counties from the Warning). The growing \nseason will end across our remaining counties tomorrow, nearly 2 \nweeks after the climatological first freeze of the season.",
